	(18, 1, '2024-05-22 02:55:49', '2024-05-22 02:55:49', '<!-- wp:image {"id":170,"sizeSlug":"large","linkDestination":"none"} -->\n<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/DSC_0261-1024x683.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-170"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Eggplant</strong>&nbsp;is one of the world’s most important vegetables and a staple in some South and Southeast Asian countries. In the Philippines, eggplant or talong is the leading vegetable crop in terms of volume (248,000 metric tons) and area of production (14,000 hectares) based on the 2022 Philippine Statistics Authority report. Eggplant farming also offers huge income potential for resource-poor farmers in many provinces in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For years, eggplant&nbsp;<strong>production</strong>&nbsp;in the Philippines suffered huge<strong>&nbsp;losses of up to 73%</strong>&nbsp;annually due to its most devastating insect pest, the&nbsp;<strong>eggplant fruit and shoot borer (EFSB)</strong>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The female moths of EFSB usually deposit eggs on the underside of the leaves which then develop into larvae. These larvae feed on soft leaf tissues and tunnels through emerging shoots and immature fruits.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To prevent severe losses due to EFSB infestation and damage, farmers spray their eggplant crops with cocktails of chemical insecticides at least 4 times a week, or up to 80 times per cropping season. According to studies, eggplant farmers spend 20-40% of the total production cost for chemical insecticides alone. This practice is costly, unsustainable, and harmful to farmers and their communities, consumers, and the environment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:block {"ref":472} /-->', 'Eggplant Production in the Philippines', '', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'eggplant-production-in-the-philippines', '', '', '2024-06-03 14:17:03', '2024-06-03 06:17:03', '', 0, 'http://localhost/?p=18', 0, 'post', '', 0),
	(20, 1, '2024-05-22 02:52:49', '2024-05-22 02:52:49', '<!-- wp:image {"id":19,"width":"516px","height":"auto","sizeSlug":"large","linkDestination":"none","style":{"color":{}}} -->\n<figure class="wp-block-image size-large is-resized"><img src="http://localhost/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/IMG_7881-1024x768.jpeg" alt="" class="wp-image-19" style="width:516px;height:auto"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->', 'Eggplant Production in the Philippines', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '18-revision-v1', '', '', '2024-05-22 02:52:49', '2024-05-22 02:52:49', '', 18, 'http://localhost/?p=20', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
	(21, 1, '2024-05-22 02:55:49', '2024-05-22 02:55:49', '<!-- wp:cover {"url":"http://localhost/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/IMG_7881-1024x768.jpeg","id":19,"dimRatio":50} -->\n<div class="wp-block-cover"><span aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-cover__background has-background-dim"></span><img class="wp-block-cover__image-background wp-image-19" alt="" src="http://localhost/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/IMG_7881-1024x768.jpeg" data-object-fit="cover"/><div class="wp-block-cover__inner-container"><!-- wp:paragraph {"align":"center","fontSize":"large"} -->\n<p class="has-text-align-center has-large-font-size">IIlocos</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div></div>\n<!-- /wp:cover -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Eggplant</strong>&nbsp;is one of the world’s most important vegetables and a staple in some South and Southeast Asian countries. In the Philippines, eggplant or talong is the leading vegetable crop in terms of volume (248,000 metric tons) and area of production (14,000 hectares) based on the 2022 Philippine Statistics Authority report. Eggplant farming also offers huge income potential for resource-poor farmers in many provinces in the country.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For years, eggplant&nbsp;<strong>production</strong>&nbsp;in the Philippines suffered huge<strong>&nbsp;losses of up to 73%</strong>&nbsp;annually due to its most devastating insect pest, the&nbsp;<strong>eggplant fruit and shoot borer (EFSB)</strong>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The female moths of EFSB usually deposit eggs on the underside of the leaves which then develop into larvae. These larvae feed on soft leaf tissues and tunnels through emerging shoots and immature fruits.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To prevent severe losses due to EFSB infestation and damage, farmers spray their eggplant crops with cocktails of chemical insecticides at least 4 times a week, or up to 80 times per cropping season. According to studies, eggplant farmers spend 20-40% of the total production cost for chemical insecticides alone. This practice is costly, unsustainable, and harmful to farmers and their communities, consumers, and the environment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Eggplant Production in the Philippines', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '18-revision-v1', '', '', '2024-05-22 02:55:49', '2024-05-22 02:55:49', '', 18, 'http://localhost/?p=21', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
